{"name":"Gold Flame","hex":"#b44f22","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"flame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible part of fire; a stream of burning vapour or gas, emitting light and heat.","A romantic partner or lover in a usually short-lived but passionate affair."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brilliant reddish orange-gold colour, like that of a flame."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flame"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flame","blocks":[{"text":"A flame is the visible, gaseous part of a fire. It is caused by a highly exothermic chemical reaction made in a thin zone. When flames are hot enough to have ionized gaseous components of sufficient density, they are then considered plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/330px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame","wikidata":"Q235544"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/500px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/1280px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/500px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flames of charcoal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADancingFlames.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/1280px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spectrum of the blue (premixed, i.e., complete combustion) flame from a butane torch showing molecular radical band emission and Swan bands. Virtually all the light produced is in the blue to green region of the spectrum below about 565 nanometers, accounting for the bluish color of sootless hydrocarbon flames.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/1280px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different flame types of a Bunsen burner depend on oxygen supply. On the left a rich fuel with no premixed oxygen produces a yellow sooty diffusion flame; on the right a lean fully oxygen premixed flame produces no soot and the flame color is produced by molecular radicals, especially CH and C2 band emission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/1280px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flame test for sodium. The yellow color in this gas flame does not arise from the black-body emission of soot particles (as the flame is clearly a blue premixed complete combustion flame) but instead comes from the spectral line emission of sodium atoms, specifically the very intense sodium D lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlametest--Na.swn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In zero-G, convection does not carry the hot combustion products away from the fuel source, resulting in a spherical flame front.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACandlespace.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/500px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/1280px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/500px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGold-crystals.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Beowulf_-_gold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Beowulf_-_gold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The word gold in the Beowulf manuscript","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_gold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/500px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par